<section class="shell band">
  <div class="split">
    <div>
      <h2>Why outside, and why side by side</h2>
      <p>Sitting face to face is a particular kind of pressure. Walking removes it. You are
      looking ahead rather than at someone looking at you, the pace gives the conversation
      somewhere to go, and the body is doing something while the hard material is in the
      air.</p>
      <p>That suits the work here, which is body-based to begin with. Somatic Experiencing
      tracks sensation and the movements a threat response never finished, and movement is not
      a metaphor in that frame. Walking is also an easier place for some people to say a thing
      out loud for the first time.</p>
      <h3>It tends to suit</h3>
      <ul class="plain">
        <li>People who talk more easily in motion than in a chair.</li>
        <li>Anxiety that sits in the body and settles with movement.</li>
        <li>Flatness and depression, where getting outside is itself part of the work.</li>
        <li>Anyone who finds an hour of eye contact harder than the topic.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>It is not for everyone</h3>
      <p>A trail is a public place, and your confidentiality is maintained there exactly as it
      is in a room. Routes, timing and how conversation is handled when other people pass are
      chosen with that in mind, and the specifics are settled in the consult and the first online
      sessions rather than assumed.</p>
      <p>Sessions are weather dependent and in central Texas that is not a formality. If the
      weather makes walking a bad idea, the session is rescheduled rather than pushed through.
      Online sessions remain the default for most of the work, with walking as part of it rather
      than all of it.</p>
      <p>Walk sessions are for adults, 18 and over. The practice does not work with young
      children, and these sessions are not offered to anyone under 18.</p>
      <h3>With horses</h3>
      <p>Equine-assisted sessions run near Dripping Springs after the same consult and one to
      three online sessions, for an additional fee. Horses respond to what your nervous system
      is doing rather than what you intend, which makes them a direct teacher of presence.
      <a href="/for-therapists/retreats-training/">Equine work for therapists</a> is separate
      and runs through the training side of the practice.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

